\begin{abstract}
Electric vehicle (EV) adoption is accelerating, yet charging cost variability remains a practical barrier to efficient operation. In many deployments, charging decisions are still made based on convenience without forecasting support, which leads to avoidable high-cost sessions. This paper presents a machine learning framework for EV charging pattern optimization that predicts session cost and converts predictions into actionable recommendations. The approach is session-centric and does not depend on proprietary Battery Management System (BMS) internals, improving portability across vehicle models and charging infrastructures. The framework integrates data preprocessing, leakage-aware temporal validation, feature engineering, model training, evaluation, and recommendation generation in a single reproducible pipeline. Historical charging sessions are used to train nonlinear regression models using Random Forest and XGBoost, while preserving realistic deployment assumptions through chronological train--test splitting. Beyond prediction, an optimization module runs what-if simulations across charging hour and charger type alternatives to select lower-cost options. A Streamlit interface provides interactive decision support. Experiments on 1,320 charging sessions evaluate the system using battery-health metrics---battery degradation, capacity retention, charging efficiency, and thermal stress---and show consistent improvements over baseline approaches. The study offers a practical, deployable foundation for user-centric EV charging optimization, with clear paths for extensions such as richer tariff data and multi-objective optimization.
\end{abstract}

