\section{INTRODUCTION}
In the move towards sustainable transport systems, the adoption of Electric Vehicles (EVs) in the private and commercial sectors is being expedited. However, the economics of charging is still one of the most important operational factors in the use of EVs. The cost of charging is affected by several factors, such as charging time, type of charging, state of charge, environmental factors, and the local tariff structure. As such, the same energy demand may result in varying charging costs depending on the context in which the charging is done.

In the real world, charging is done without the aid of predictive tools. Users simply choose the charging time and type of charging available, depending on their convenience rather than the cost implications of the charging session. For time-variable tariff systems, this may result in repetitive high-cost charging sessions. As the use of EVs increases, there is an urgent need for tools that can predict the charging cost in the near future, such that alternative charging options can be made available in time before the start of the charging session.

In the context of EV intelligence, the traditional intelligence layer, such as the Battery Management System (BMS), plays an important role in ensuring the safety of the battery, state of charge, and overall battery protection. However, the BMS is not likely to play an important role in optimizing the cost of the charging session at the user level, since such data is usually not available in an open format. As such, there is an urgent need for an alternative approach that is informed by the charging session data available in the open domain.

This work presents a machine learning framework for EV charging pattern optimization. The framework predicts charging session cost using structured historical data and then uses the trained model to evaluate alternative charging decisions. The objective is practical: provide cost-aware charging recommendations without relying on proprietary control interfaces.

The proposed pipeline follows a reproducible sequence: data ingestion, preprocessing and validation, leakage-aware temporal splitting, feature engineering, model training, model evaluation, artifact persistence, and recommendation generation. A chronological train--test split is used to avoid temporal leakage and to match real deployment behavior, where models are trained on historical sessions and applied to future sessions.

Feature engineering is designed to capture multiple cost drivers. The system constructs SOC-derived features, temporal indicators, efficiency metrics, interaction terms, and user-level historical aggregates. User aggregates are computed from training data only to prevent leakage. Categorical transformations and final feature alignment are also training-aware, ensuring consistent schema during inference.

Two non-linear regression models that are used in this study include Random Forest and XGBoost models. These models can be applied to tabular data, which may include different features. The evaluation is reported using battery-centric performance measures aligned with the energy-management objective: battery degradation (primary), capacity retention, charging efficiency, and thermal stress index.

Prediction alone does not provide operational value unless users can act on it. Therefore, the framework includes an optimization module that performs what-if simulations for each session. It varies charging hour and charger type under fixed session constraints, predicts cost for each scenario, and returns a lower-cost recommendation. The module also flags inefficient sessions and estimates potential savings.

For deployment, the framework is integrated into a Streamlit application. The application loads serialized artifacts, receives user input, performs inference, and displays recommendations in an interpretable format. Since the system uses session-level observables and avoids BMS dependencies, integration overhead is low and portability is high.

The main contributions of this work are summarized below:
1) an end-to-end, leakage-aware machine learning pipeline for EV charging cost prediction;
2) integration of forecasting and optimization in a single decision-support flow;
3) deployable implementation through an interactive user interface;
4) transparent evaluation under realistic temporal validation.

This paper is organized as follows. Section II reviews related work in EV charging analytics and secure forecasting practices. Section III presents the system architecture. 


\section{LITERATURE SURVEY}
The scope of electric vehicle (EV) analytics research involves battery state estimation, charging control, grid-level coordination, and user-level forecasting. The early trends of electric vehicle analytics focused on battery estimation and charging control. In this regard, robust battery state estimation was performed through Kalman filter methods, considering various uncertain conditions of the sensor readings [1]. Later, the scope of electric vehicle analytics was extended to state-of-health forecasting of the battery to predict the degradation of the battery and estimate the battery lifetime [2]. These trends of electric vehicle analytics research do not consider the optimization of the charging session cost from the user’s point of view.
In parallel, there has been considerable research into grid-oriented charging optimization. Sortomme and El-Sharkawi developed optimal charging strategies that take into account grid and peak considerations [3]. Gan, Topcu, and Low proposed charging protocols that are decentralized and have excellent scalability [4]. Though these charging protocols are effective in charging scenarios, their objectives are largely centered around network stability rather than cost recommendations. Pricing strategies and tariff structures also influence charging behavior and overall cost outcomes, which motivates session-level cost analysis [5].
For tabular EV datasets, ensemble machine learning models are commonly adopted. Random Forest is frequently used as a robust baseline for noisy data. Gradient boosting methods often provide stronger predictive performance in nonlinear settings. XGBoost, in particular, has become a widely used method for structured prediction tasks due to efficiency and regularization support [6].
Reinforcement learning is also used in adaptive charging in dynamic pricing systems [7]. Although reinforcement learning is promising in decision-making, it is often required in stable environments with well-designed reward systems, as well as in the deployment of the systems. However, in many charging systems, supervised learning-based forecasting with scenario simulation is easier to validate, interpret, and integrate.
A critical methodological issue is validation design. For temporally dependent data, random splitting can leak future patterns and inflate reported performance. Bergmeir and Benitez emphasized the importance of time-aware evaluation for forecasting tasks [8]. In EV charging, where tariffs and user behavior can drift over time, chronological splitting is necessary for realistic generalization assessment.
Recent literature has begun moving from pure prediction to decision-support workflows. However, many studies still stop at offline metrics and do not provide an operational recommendation layer. As a result, a practical gap remains between model development and deployable user assistance.
This work addresses that gap through an integrated framework that combines leakage-aware cost forecasting with an optimization engine for session-level recommendations. The approach is complementary to battery-control and grid-level methods. It targets user-centric charging efficiency through a deployable and interpretable architecture.

\subsection*{Research Gaps}
Despite strong progress in EV analytics, several gaps remain in the existing literature:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Limited transition from prediction to action:} Many studies report forecasting accuracy but do not convert predictions into practical charging recommendations that users can directly apply.
    \item \textbf{Insufficient leakage-aware evaluation:} A number of prior works still rely on random data splits, which may overestimate performance for temporally evolving charging behavior.
    \item \textbf{Weak user-centric optimization focus:} Existing methods are often grid-centric or battery-centric, with less emphasis on minimizing session-level charging cost for end users.
    \item \textbf{Low deployment portability:} Several approaches depend on proprietary vehicle internals or tightly coupled infrastructure data, reducing real-world adaptability across EV platforms.
    \item \textbf{Fragmented pipelines:} Prior studies frequently address isolated stages (modeling only), while reproducible end-to-end flows from preprocessing to recommendation serving are less common.
\end{itemize}

\section{SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE}

The EV Charging Pattern Optimization system, which is being proposed, is a machine learning-based system described as a modular, end-to-end solution that processes raw data related to charging sessions and then converts this data into a usable, user-friendly solution. The system is described as being reproducible, leakage-aware, interpretable, and deployable, and it does not require data from a specific Battery Management System, instead relying on features and behaviors to estimate charging session costs and to determine optimization potential.

From a bird's-eye view, this system is best described as a seven-layer system consisting of data ingestion/storage, data preprocessing/validation, temporal splitting/feature engineering, model training/model selection, model evaluation/artifact generation, optimization/recognition engine, and application serving layers, with the application layer being a Streamlit-based solution.

