\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Data Ingestion and Storage Layer}: 
We start with a clean dataset that is well organized, including all the relevant information that pertains to the electric vehicle (EV) charging sessions. The dataset includes a separate entry for each EV charging session, including user information, vehicle information, charging station information, timestamps, state of charge, environmental conditions, energy consumption, and cost of charging, which acts as the target for this model.

The goal here is to maintain simplicity to ensure that the experiment can be replicated easily. The components utilize the same source file for data ingestion to ensure that there are no inconsistencies that may occur due to disparate data sets. This ensures proper ingestion of the file and also determines the file paths to ensure that the system can always recognize a uniform schema for each entry.

There is also an output component that helps to differentiate between raw and processed data to ensure traceability and to allow for retraining from a known state.

\item \textbf{Preprocessing and Validation Layer}: 

There is also a unique component for data preprocessing in this layer. This layer not only preprocesses the data for training but also validates the data concurrently.

This layer is very important in the experiment because cost models for charging stations are very sensitive to data validity and to invalid operational values. This architecture excels here because the same process applies to all model experiments as well as to future inference.

\item \textbf{Temporal Splitting and Feature Engineering Layer
}: 
We'll first implement time-aware data splitting to ensure no leakage, as well as mimicking the model's behavior when deployed, including training on historical sessions, testing on future sessions, and understanding charging patterns over time.

For the feature engineering layer, we'll utilize a dedicated feature engineering module to build a set of composable features. This includes SOC-based features, time-based features, efficiency-based features, interaction features, and user aggregates, all built on top of the training data.

For user aggregates, we'll build these strictly on top of the training data to ensure no leakage, then append the test/inference data afterward.

For our categorical features, we'll implement leakage-resistant, training-aware encoding for our features. The final feature matrix is then aligned with our predefined feature column contract to ensure that features are identical across our training and inference settings.

\item \textbf{Model Training and Selection Layer}:  
This layer is focused on training regression models that can be specialized according to our specific problem statement. This layer includes two regression models, which are a Random Forest Regressor model and an XGBoost Regressor model.

Both models are suitable for our non-linear tabular data problem, as well as the feature importance, which is applicable according to our problem statement.

This module is also able to handle regular training, including optional hyperparameter tuning, should we need to implement this.

For model selection, we'll rely on our evaluation metrics to determine which model is best for our problem statement. This is also able to compare models on our validation and/or test data to determine which model is best for our problem statement, based on our evaluation criteria.

The training run also includes model artifacts and features, which are utilized during training, and features are serialized to disk to ensure consistency across inference, even if code changes are made.

\item \textbf{Evaluation and Artifact Generation Layer}: 
The evaluation layer provides battery-health metrics, which are exported in machine-readable JSON format for easier auditing. The system also generates diagnostic visuals for validation and interpretability, as well as for the purpose of explaining the model results to the research community.

The evaluation layer uses a modular structure to ensure that the objective model evaluation is independent of the training code. This is beneficial from the perspective of scientific communication, as well as future benchmarking.

\item \textbf{Optimization and Recommendation Engine Layer}:
The optimization module is independent of the training module. This module uses the model as a predictor, while the what-if transformations are applied to the features that need to be considered. This ensures that the model can be updated without modifying the code of the optimization module, as well as the optimization policies without modifying the predictor code.

\item \textbf{Application Serving Layer}:
The application serving layer is built upon the Streamlit layer. This layer is the user interface of the application. This layer fetches the stored artifacts, handles the input, preprocesses the input, creates the features, and shows the output, such as the cost, as well as the strategies that need to be adopted. Caching is used so that the model does not need to be loaded each time.
\end{itemize}

\section{EXPERIMENTAL SETUP}

The experiment will be performed by comparing the optimized energy management system, hereafter referred to as \textbf{Evo}, with three different baselines: XGBoost, Random Forest, and a baseline model. Although battery degradation is the key performance indicator, we will also be looking at capacity retention, charging efficiency, and thermal stress as well.

\subsection{Data and Features}
The data will be collected on EV charging sessions, vehicle properties such as battery size and vehicle age, charging session properties such as charging duration and charging rate, and other relevant properties such as time of day, day of week, and temperature, to name a few. Before any modeling takes place, data preprocessing will be performed to ensure data quality and realism.
Feature engineering will be based on battery characteristics like SOC gained during charging sessions, charging efficiency, cost factors, time factors like peak and off-peak hours, and user factors aggregated from the training data.

\subsection{Models and Optimization Strategy}
Random Forest and XGBoost will be the two baseline models used for the experiment. Evo is based on a predictive machine learning system with optimization logic that selects charging time and charger type while minimizing degradation and stress and maximizing efficiency. The baseline model represents standard charging behavior without decision support; each session is evaluated using the recorded attributes (charger type, charging rate, SOC window, and ambient temperature) without modifying charging time or charger selection. This mirrors user-driven charging based on convenience rather than battery-health considerations.

The no-optimization baseline represents standard charging behavior without any decision support or model-driven recommendations. Each session is evaluated using the recorded attributes (charger type, charging rate, SOC window, and ambient temperature) without modifying charging time or charger selection. This mirrors a real-world scenario in which users charge based on convenience rather than cost or battery-health considerations, and it serves as the reference case for all session-health comparisons.

\subsection{Training and Evaluation Protocol}
We follow this approach: train on the chronological training set and evaluate on the held-out set. In addition, we compare the performance of Evo with XGBoost, Random Forest, and the baseline model on the same test data. The primary measure of performance is battery degradation, as this directly affects battery health. In addition, capacity retention, charging efficiency, and a thermal stress index are also used as metrics, both in absolute as well as relative terms.

\subsection{Result Interpretation Strategy}
We measure the performance of the model based on the ability of Evo to perform better than all the baselines under the same test conditions. The fact that improvements are seen across all metrics indicates that Evo, which combines prediction and optimization, performs better than the baselines, which only rely on prediction. The improvements seen in battery degradation and thermal stress are a clear indication of the model’s ability to ensure battery health without compromising efficiency.


\section{RESULTS AND ANALYSIS}

The study aimed at implementing Evo to improve battery condition and overall vehicle efficiency. Battery degradation was the main measure, but the study also explored other battery performance measures. Evo was compared to three other options: XGBoost, Random Forest, and the baseline model. Across all measures used in the study, Evo performed best.

\subsection{Battery degradation as the key metric}
The focus of the study was on battery degradation since it is a key factor in determining how quickly the battery loses its ability to function. The lower the degradation value, the better the performance of the battery. Evo achieved the best degradation value of 0.021. XGBoost recorded 0.024, Random Forest recorded 0.027, and the baseline model recorded 0.033. From the results above, Evo performed best compared to the other three methods used in the study. In addition, Evo was 0.012 lower than the baseline model, which is a 36.36\% reduction. This indicates that Evo is effective in dealing with the factors that affect battery degradation.
