\subsection{Competition with the Machine Learning Methods}
The study was not limited to the optimization method but compared Evo with the machine learning baselines. Evo was 0.003 lower than XGBoost's battery degradation value of 0.024, which is a 12.5\% reduction. Evo was 0.006 lower than Random Forest's battery degradation value of 0.027, which is a 22.22\% reduction.

From the results above, Evo performed best compared to the machine learning methods. This indicates that Evo is effective in dealing with the intricate relations that exist in the battery's operations, the charging process, and the degradation process.

\subsection{Capacity Retention Analysis}
Capacity retention is defined as the percentage of the battery’s capacity retained after a certain number of charge and discharge cycles. In other words, the more capacity retention, the longer the battery will last and the more reliable the energy system will be.
Evo has a capacity retention of 93.4\%, whereas the baseline has a capacity retention of 88.9\%. This is an absolute improvement of 4.5\% and a relative improvement of 5.06\%. Capacity retention is critical in an electric vehicle since it determines how far you can drive and how reliable the system is. The improvement in Evo suggests that the optimization technique reduces the usual stress on the battery.
Across all models, capacity retention follows the same ordering as degradation: Evo is highest, followed by XGBoost, Random Forest, and the no-optimization baseline.

\subsection{Charging Efficiency Evaluation}
Charging efficiency is defined as the amount of energy put into the battery when charging and how it is utilized in the system. In other words, charging efficiency is a measure of how efficiently the energy is utilized in the system.
Evo has a charging efficiency of 0.944, whereas the baseline has a charging efficiency of 0.896. This is an absolute improvement of 0.048 and a relative improvement of 5.36\%. An improvement in charging efficiency is critical since it will make the system more efficient and more reliable in terms of energy sustainability.
The comparative results show Evo with the highest charging efficiency, followed by XGBoost and Random Forest, while the no-optimization baseline remains lowest.

\subsection{Thermal Stress Analysis}
Temperature is critical in determining the performance and lifespan of the battery. In this regard, Evo utilizes a thermal stress index as a measure of how well the model regulates temperature in the system. Evo has a thermal stress of 0.27, whereas the baseline has a thermal stress of 0.42. This is an absolute improvement of 0.15 and a relative improvement of 35.71\%. The reduction in thermal stress is critical since it will make the system more efficient in the utilization of energy and more reliable in terms of sustainability.

